/abs/2505.06012).\n\n                                √\n20.24. Does the group SL2 (Z[ 2]) admit a faithful transitive amenable action?\n   This is the same as asking if it admits any co-amenable subgroup except the finite\nindex subgroups.                                               Y. Glasner, N. Monod\n\n20.25. Let n = k 2 . Let σ ∈ Sn be the product of k disjoint cycles, of lengths\n1, 3, 5, . . . , 2k − 1. Find the limiting proportion of odd entries in the σ-column of the\ncharacter table of Sn .\n    For some background, see pages 1005–1006 in (D. Gluck, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc.,\n147 (2019), 1005–1011).                                                            D. Gluck\n\n20.26. Let ϕ be the Euler totient function. Does there exist a constant a > 0 such\nthat |Aut(G)| ⩾ ϕ(|G|)a\n    a) for every finite group G?\n    b) for every finite nilpotent group G?\n   If such a constant a exists, then a ⩽ 40\n                                          41 (J. González-Sánchez, A. Jaikin-Zapirain,\nForum Math. Sigma, 3, Article ID e7, 11 p., electronic only (2015)). Also cf. Archive\n12.77.                                         J. González-Sánchez, A. Jaikin-Zapirain\n\n∗ 20.27. Let G be a finite group, p a prime number, and let |xG | denote the maximum\n                                                                 p\npower of p that divides the class size of an element x ∈ G. Suppose that there exists\na p-element g ∈ G such that |g G |p = maxx∈G |xG |p . Is it true that G has a normal\np-complement?\n    A partial answer is in (https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.03641).          I. B. Gorshkov\n  ∗ No, not necessarily, a counterexample is given by SmallGroup(192,945) (B. Sam-\nbale, Letter of 16 February 2022).\n\n20.28. Let L be a non-abelian finite simple group, and let H(L) = M (L).L be the\nuniversal perfect central extension, where M (L) is the Schur multiplier of L. Suppose\nthat G is a finite group such that the set of class sizes of G is the same as the set of\nclass sizes of H(L). Is it true that G ≃ H(L) × A, where A is an abelian group?\n    This is proved for L = Alt5 in (J. Algebra Appl., 21, no. 11 (2022), Article ID\n2250226, 8 p.).                                                          I. B. Gorshkov\n\frendered_page: /tmp/kourovka-20-21-page.png\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
